    <header class="article-hero"><div class="container article-head"><nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Test Before You Build</a><span>›</span><span>Customer discovery interviews</span></nav><p class="eyebrow">Interview guide</p><h1>Customer discovery interviews: questions, process and evidence</h1><p class="standfirst">Customer discovery interviews help you understand how potential buyers or users behave before you build. The value comes from relevant participants, neutral questions, concrete past examples and disciplined analysis—not from asking whether people like your idea.</p><div class="byline"><img src="/kari-jaaskelainen.jpg" width="72" height="72" alt="Dr. Kari Jääskeläinen" /><div><strong>By <a href="/about-kari-jaaskelainen/">Dr. Kari Jääskeläinen</a></strong><span>Market validation, commercialisation and customer discovery</span><time datetime={published}>Published and updated 17 September 2026</time></div></div><aside class="scope-summary"><strong>The short answer</strong><p>Ask participants to reconstruct recent real events. Explore the problem, consequences, current alternatives, triggers, buying process and barriers. Listen more than you speak, probe without suggesting an answer and compare evidence across interviews before making a decision.</p></aside></div></header>
    <div class="container article-layout"><aside class="toc" aria-label="On this page"><strong>On this page</strong><a href="#purpose">Purpose</a><a href="#process">Interview process</a><a href="#questions">Questions to ask</a><a href="#avoid">Questions to avoid</a><a href="#evidence">Analyse evidence</a><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></aside><div class="article-body">
      <section id="purpose"><h2>Customer discovery is research, not a disguised sales call</h2><p>A customer discovery interview examines how a defined person experiences a problem and makes decisions around it. It should reveal what happens today: the trigger, workflow, consequences, alternatives, buying criteria, decision-makers and obstacles to change.</p><p>The interviewer begins with assumptions but does not ask the participant to confirm them. A positive reaction to a proposed idea is easy to obtain and hard to interpret. A detailed account of a recent event is more useful because it exposes actual behaviour, constraints and trade-offs.</p><blockquote>The goal is not to make the participant believe in the idea. The goal is to understand whether the evidence gives you a reason to believe in it.</blockquote><h3>What interviews can and cannot tell you</h3><p>Interviews can show whether relevant people repeatedly experience the problem, how seriously they treat it, what they already do, why alternatives succeed or fail and what must happen before adoption. They can substantially reduce the risk of building around false assumptions.</p><p>They do not guarantee future sales. Strong market validation may combine interview findings with behavioural evidence such as a pilot, pre-sale, procurement step, signed commitment or completed purchase. The appropriate next test depends on the decision and the maturity of the idea.</p></section>
      <section id="process"><h2>A practical customer discovery interview process</h2><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Define the decision.</strong><span>State what investment or direction the research must inform.</span></li><li><strong>List the critical assumptions.</strong><span>Identify what must be true about the problem, customer, current behaviour and buying conditions.</span></li><li><strong>Recruit relevant participants.</strong><span>Use explicit criteria and distinguish users, buyers and other decision-makers.</span></li><li><strong>Create a short interview guide.</strong><span>Use consistent themes while leaving room for follow-up questions.</span></li><li><strong>Pilot the guide.</strong><span>Remove jargon, double questions and wording that reveals the desired answer.</span></li><li><strong>Set expectations and obtain consent.</strong><span>Explain the purpose, duration, confidentiality and handling of notes or transcripts.</span></li><li><strong>Conduct the interview.</strong><span>Begin broadly, reconstruct specific events and probe important details without pitching.</span></li><li><strong>Document evidence promptly.</strong><span>Preserve the participant’s meaning and separate what was said from your interpretation.</span></li><li><strong>Compare interviews systematically.</strong><span>Look for recurring patterns, meaningful differences, exceptions and missing evidence.</span></li><li><strong>Connect findings to the decision.</strong><span>Decide what is supported, weakened, unresolved and what should be tested next.</span></li></ol><p>Participant quality is part of this process. See <a href="/b2b-customer-research/">how to define, find and recruit B2B interview participants</a>.</p></section>
      <aside class="inline-cta"><p class="eyebrow">Need independent customer evidence?</p><h2>Dr. Kari recruits, interviews and analyses the intended market.</h2><p>The standard validation project includes 20 interviews, transcripts, evidence analysis, a written report, recommended changes and a BUILD, CHANGE or KILL recommendation.</p><a class="button" href="/market-validation-services-pricing/">See services and pricing</a></aside>
      <section id="questions"><h2>Customer discovery interview questions that produce useful evidence</h2><p>Treat this as a topic guide, not a questionnaire that must be recited word for word. Choose questions that match the commercial decision and use neutral follow-up probes.</p><div class="question-grid"><div><h3>Context and role</h3><ul><li>Can you describe your role in this process?</li><li>Who else is involved, and what is each person responsible for?</li><li>How often does this situation occur?</li></ul></div><div><h3>A recent real event</h3><ul><li>Tell me about the last time this happened.</li><li>What triggered the situation?</li><li>What did you do first? What happened next?</li></ul></div><div><h3>Problem and consequences</h3><ul><li>Which part was most difficult?</li><li>What did the problem cost in time, money, risk or missed opportunity?</li><li>What happens if nothing changes?</li></ul></div><div><h3>Current alternatives</h3><ul><li>How do you handle this today?</li><li>What have you already tried?</li><li>What works well, and where does the current approach fail?</li></ul></div><div><h3>Decision and buying process</h3><ul><li>What would trigger a search for another approach?</li><li>Who defines requirements, approves the purchase and controls the budget?</li><li>What evidence would they need?</li></ul></div><div><h3>Barriers and commitment</h3><ul><li>What could prevent a change?</li><li>What would implementation require?</li><li>What concrete next step would be realistic?</li></ul></div></div><h3>Neutral probes that deepen an answer</h3><ul><li>Can you give me a specific example?</li><li>What happened next?</li><li>How did you decide that?</li><li>Who was involved?</li><li>What made that difficult?</li><li>How did you measure the result?</li><li>Is that typical, or was this case unusual?</li></ul><p>A useful follow-up stays close to the participant’s answer. It asks for detail without supplying an explanation for them.</p></section>
      <section id="avoid"><h2>Customer discovery questions to avoid—or rewrite</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Avoid</th><th>Why it is weak</th><th>Ask instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Would you buy this?</td><td>Invites a hypothetical, socially easy answer</td><td>Tell me about the last time you paid to solve this problem.</td></tr><tr><td>Do you think this is a good idea?</td><td>Measures politeness and opinion, not behaviour</td><td>How do you handle this situation today?</td></tr><tr><td>Wouldn’t this save you time?</td><td>Contains the desired answer</td><td>Where does time go in the current process?</td></tr><tr><td>How much would you pay?</td><td>Price is abstract before context and value are known</td><td>What budget funds the current approach, and how is it approved?</td></tr><tr><td>What features do you want?</td><td>Moves too early into solution design</td><td>What outcome are you trying to achieve, and what prevents it?</td></tr><tr><td>Why don’t you use X?</td><td>Can sound accusatory and invite rationalisation</td><td>What alternatives did you consider, and what happened?</td></tr><tr><td>Is this a major problem?</td><td>Forces a vague rating</td><td>What were the consequences the last time it occurred?</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Do not pitch before you understand the present</h3><p>Showing the idea changes the conversation. Participants begin reacting to your framing instead of describing their world. If the study also needs concept feedback, complete the discovery portion first, mark the transition and analyse pre-concept and post-concept evidence separately.</p><h3>Listen for more than praise</h3><p>Notice workarounds, spending, delays, escalation, switching attempts and access to decision-makers. Also record indifference, low urgency, adequate existing solutions, procurement barriers and contradictions. Negative evidence is not a failed interview; it may prevent a failed investment.</p></section>
      <section id="evidence"><h2>Turn customer interviews into decision-ready evidence</h2><p>Do not analyse interviews by collecting memorable quotations or counting how many people sounded positive. Use the same evidence framework across transcripts and preserve differences between participant roles and market segments.</p><div class="evidence-note"><strong>Separate three layers</strong><p><strong>Observation:</strong> what the participant reported or described. <strong>Interpretation:</strong> what the team believes it means. <strong>Decision implication:</strong> what, if anything, should change because of it.</p></div><h3>A simple analysis framework</h3><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Prepare the material.</strong><span>Check transcripts, context, participant role and relevant segment.</span></li><li><strong>Code decision-relevant evidence.</strong><span>Group passages under problems, consequences, alternatives, triggers, buying criteria, barriers and commitments.</span></li><li><strong>Compare within and between groups.</strong><span>Do not hide differences between users, buyers, markets or use cases.</span></li><li><strong>Test each critical assumption.</strong><span>Mark evidence that supports, weakens or leaves it unresolved.</span></li><li><strong>Investigate contradictions.</strong><span>Determine whether they reveal an exception, a separate segment or a flawed assumption.</span></li><li><strong>Assess sufficiency.</strong><span>Ask whether more interviews are likely to change the decision; see <a href="/how-many-customer-interviews/">how many customer interviews are enough</a>.</span></li><li><strong>Recommend action.</strong><span>Translate findings into BUILD, CHANGE or KILL, with limitations and the next evidence required.</span></li></ol><h3>Evidence is stronger when it is specific and consequential</h3><div class="comparison-grid"><div><h3>Weaker signal</h3><ul><li>general interest;</li><li>future intention;</li><li>feature suggestions;</li><li>agreement after a pitch;</li><li>an isolated positive quotation.</li></ul></div><div><h3>Stronger signal</h3><ul><li>a recent concrete event;</li><li>repeated costly consequences;</li><li>active workarounds or spending;</li><li>a clear buying process and trigger;</li><li>a credible next commitment.</li></ul></div></div><p>For the wider method mix, read <a href="/market-validation/">Market validation: process, methods and examples</a>.</p></section>
      <section id="faq"><p class="eyebrow">Frequently asked questions</p><h2>Questions about customer discovery interviews</h2><div class="faq-list">{faqs.map(({question,answer})=><details><summary>{question}</summary><p>{answer}</p></details>)}</div></section>
      <section class="final-cta"><p class="eyebrow eyebrow-light">Independent customer discovery</p><h2>Make the next investment decision from evidence, not encouragement.</h2><p>The standard project includes target definition, approximately 30–40 suitable prospects for client approval, recruitment, 20 interviews, transcripts, evidence analysis, a written report, recommended changes and a BUILD, CHANGE or KILL recommendation.</p><p>Fixed price: €2,900 + VAT. Typical timeline: approximately 2–4 weeks. Larger and multi-segment projects can be scoped separately.</p><a class="button button-light" href="/#contact">Submit your idea</a></section>
      <section class="author-card"><img src="/kari-jaaskelainen.jpg" width="160" height="160" alt="Dr. Kari Jääskeläinen" loading="lazy" /><div><p class="eyebrow">About the author</p><h2>Dr. Kari Jääskeläinen</h2><p>Dr. Kari Jääskeläinen is an entrepreneur, business leader and consultant with experience in market validation, technology commercialisation, business development and sales. He has worked on around 200 commercialisation and consulting projects and has made approximately 2,000 sales calls a year.</p><a href="/about-kari-jaaskelainen/">Read about Dr. Kari Jääskeläinen’s experience →</a></div></section>
      <section class="sources"><h2>Sources and further reading</h2><ol><li>Harvard Business School Rock Center for Entrepreneurship. <a href="https://entrepreneurship.hbs.edu/Documents/Session%20Summary/HBSRock-Customer-Discovery-Final.pdf"><cite>Customer Discovery Basics</cite></a>.</li><li>DeJonckheere, M. and Vaughn, L. M. (2019). <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6910737/"><cite>Semistructured interviewing in primary care research: a balance of relationship and rigour</cite></a>.</li><li>Kallio, H. et al. (2016). <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27221824/"><cite>Systematic methodological review: developing a framework for a qualitative semi-structured interview guide</cite></a>.</li><li>Moser, A. and Korstjens, I. (2018). <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5774281/"><cite>Practical guidance to qualitative research. Part 3: Sampling, data collection and analysis</cite></a>.</li></ol></section>
